    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d196">

                <pb facs="0298" n="290" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg290"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94/2178<hi rendition="#superscript">3/18</hi>
                </note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. George W. Renchard, of the Secretary’s
                        Office, to the Chief of the Division of Far Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hamilton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-07-05">July 5, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Hamilton</hi>: The Secretary wishes you to
                    consider the significance of the oral statement communicated through Mr. Walker
                    to <pb facs="0299" n="291" xml:id="pg291"/> the effect that Japan would agree to
                    get her troops out of China within two years. The oral statement may not mean
                    anything, but on the other hand, it may be run down for the purposes of a
                    showdown.</p>
                <p>“When I talked with Hamilton, I suggested that he get in touch with Japanese
                    representatives there (Washington) and inquire specifically how their Government
                    expected to go forward with a peace settlement for the Pacific area while moving
                    in exactly the opposite direction—the truth of the Associated Press despatch
                    from Shanghai, reported in today’s paper, should either be admitted or
                    repudiated. In this same connection, Hamilton could thoroughly explore the oral
                    statement of the Ambassador in regard to getting the Japanese Army out of China
                    within two years. I hope that Hamilton will exhibit his usual firmness and also
                    cordiality in his conversation.”<anchor n="65" xml:id="d196-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">George W. Renchard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
